Note: `AVAILABLE_SKILLS` appears nowhere in the indexed code, so nothing below matches it — the results are ranked on the rest of the question. That name does not exist under that spelling; check it, or consider that the code may live in a file the index leaves out (the .github/ tree, generated output, lock files), which a `grep` would still find.

[
  {
    "path": "src/content/prompt-template.md",
    "lines": "203-210",
    "excerpt": "A skill is a set of instructions this project has written for a particular kind of
work. When the work in front of you is of a kind a skill below covers, load it
with `load_skill` and follow it in place of your own approach: it
is what this project has decided, not advice to weigh. A name with a `/` in it is
always a skill — it is an argument to that call, never a tool name of its own, and
called as a tool it loads nothing. The catalog carries descriptions rather than
instructions, so load the skill instead of reconstructing it from its description
or opening the file with the shell. Loading one counts toward no operational limit."
  },
  {
    "path": "docs/agents/overview.md",
    "lines": "25-29",
    "excerpt": "`.github/atomaton/prompt-template.md` is what every agent is told, whichever one
it is — it is passed to Atoma with `--template` on every runner invocation, and
each role prompt is placed inside it. Skills live under
`.github/atomaton/skills/**/*.md`; `.github/atomaton/README.md` says which of
those directories the template ships and which are yours outright."
  },
  {
    "path": "tests/contract/agent-prompts.test.ts",
    "lines": "205-215",
    "excerpt": "s as skills without requesting visible chain of thought", () => {
    const prompt = readFileSync("src/content/prompt-template.md", "utf8");
    // The strict spelling pins live HERE, in the release that completes the
    // pairing: this release ships the template that teaches the bare name
    // `load_skill`, and pins the atoma (v0.4.6) that dispatches it, so the two
    // halves ship as one versioned unit instead of agreeing across releases.
    expect(prompt).toContain("load_skill");
    expect(prompt).not.toContain("atoma_builtin__load_skill");
    expect(prompt).toContain("in place of your own approach");
    expect(prompt).toContain("Check the catalog again");
    expect(prompt).toC"
  }
]
